## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of integration)
## DBsection(Work)
## KEYWORDS('definite integral', 'work')
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Author(Brian Burns)
## Level(5)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('6.6')
## Problem1('1')
DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',      'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl',
    'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Numeric");

$a = 0;
$B = random(3, 5);
$c = random(1, 3);
$m = random(1, 2);
$T = $m * $B + $c;

$f = Formula("$m*x+$c")->reduce;
($D, $N) = reduce($m, 2);
$H  = Formula("$D*x^2/$N+$c*x")->reduce;
$g1 = $f->string;
Context("Fraction");
$C2 = Compute("($B)**2*$m/2+$c*$B");

$plot = Plot(
    xmin        => -1,
    xmax        =>  4.5,
    xtick_delta =>  1,
    xminor      =>  0,
    xlabel      => 'Position (ft)',
    ymin        => -1,
    ymax        =>  $f->eval(x => 4) + 1,
    ytick_delta =>  1,
    yminor      =>  0,
    ylabel          => 'Force (lb)',
    show_grid       => 0,
    rounded_corners => 1
);
$plot->add_function($g1, 'x', 0, 4, color => 'blue');

BEGIN_PGML

A variable force [`F(x)`] in the positive [`x`]-direction is graphed in the 
accompanying figure. Find the work done by the force on a particle that moves 
from [`x=0`] to [`x=[$B]`]. 

>> [!A plot of a line from (0,[$c]) to ([$B],[$B+$m*$C])!]{$plot}{300}<<

    Work Done [`=`] [_]{$C2} ft[`\cdot`]lb

END_PGML
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
With [`F(x)=[$f],\;a=0,\;b=[$B]`]

Work Done [```=\int_{a}^{b} F(x) \;dx=\int_{0}^{[$B]}  \left([$f]\right) \;dx =[$C1]\left[[$H]\right]_{0}^{[$B]}=[$C2]```]

The Work Done is [`[$C2]`] ft[`\cdot`]lb.

Note that the work done is given by an integral that represents the area under the curve in the graph. Therefore, the result could also have been obtained by finding the area of the trapezoid:

[```W =(b-a)\frac{1}{2}(y_1 +y_2)= ([$B]-0)\frac{1}{2}([$c] +[$T]) = [$C2].```]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
